* ===========================================================================
* DOES STOP_ON_ERROR BAIL OUT OF A SCAN?  -- and can it SEE the error at all?
* ===========================================================================
*
* A PROBE, NOT A REGISTERED SPEC. Not in kRegressionSpecs; runs by DOTSCRIPT.
*
* RULING (steward, 2026-09-01): "stop on error has to mean bail out." Before
* that the errorstop check lived ONLY at the script's line loop, so a SCAN body
* failing on record 2 of 4 still ran records 3 and 4 -- the check could not fire
* until ENDSCAN had returned. The check now also sits INSIDE the scan, at the
* LINE.
*
* THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS PROBE FAILED TO PROVE ANYTHING, AND THAT WAS THE
* MOST USEFUL RUN OF THE DAY. It used REPLACE on an unknown field as the
* failure. Nothing stopped. Not the scan, not the script. The reason is not the
* bail-out: it is that REPLACE'S FAILURE IS NOT AN ERROR AS FAR AS THE SYSTEM
* IS CONCERNED.
*
*   cli::cmdout::print_prefixed_message(id)  -- formats a catalogued message
*   cli::cmdout::emit_error(id, code)        -- SAME print, PLUS set_last_error
*
* Only the second moves the error generation, and only a moved generation trips
* the threshold. Measured across src/: print_prefixed_message 518 call sites,
* print_message 800, emit_error 2. STOP_ON_ERROR is a threshold over a channel
* almost nothing writes to.
*
* SO THIS PROBE HAS TWO SECTIONS AND THEY PROVE OPPOSITE THINGS.
*
*   SECTION 1 is a TRIPWIRE over the silent-failure gap. No bail is expected.
*             SCE_T1 reading .T. means all four records ran THROUGH a printed
*             failure. When REPLACE is converted to emit_error, SCE_T1 FLIPS TO
*             .F. and this section should be re-pointed deliberately rather
*             than allowed to retune itself (the IDXSTALE precedent).
*
*   SECTION 2 proves the BAIL-OUT, using a failure that really is recorded.
*             SET ERRORSTOP TO <garbage> is one of the only two paths in the
*             tree that calls set_last_error on failure (cmd_set.cpp:1609,
*             e_invalid_argument), and it returns BEFORE changing the
*             threshold, so it records an error and disturbs nothing else.
*
* THE SCRIPT IS EXPECTED TO STOP AT SECTION 2'S ENDSCAN. That is the pass, not
* a failure. The script-level stop is the second half of the ruling, and
* suppressing it to get a tidy transcript would be testing a different build.
*
* WHAT PASS LOOKS LIKE:
*
*   SCE_G1 .T.   SCE_G2 .T.
*   REPLACE: field not found.     (x4 -- section 1, printed and unrecorded)
*   ENDSCAN: 4 match(es), 4 iteration(s) over 2 line(s).
*   SCE_T1_replace_failure_is_INVISIBLE_all_rows_ran:.T.
*   ENDSCAN: STOPPED at record 1 (STOP_ON_ERROR WARNING).
*     0 record(s) completed; record 1 was partially processed.
*     Nothing is rolled back -- SCAN is not a transaction.
*   DOTSCRIPT: ...:NN: stopped (STOP_ON_ERROR WARNING)
*
* SCE_X1 MUST NOT PRINT.
*
* THEN READ THE TABLE AT THE PROMPT -- the half no marker can carry, because
* the script has already stopped:
*
*     SELECT SCERR
*     GO 1
*     TUPLE          && expect  C1 | HIT | HIT    reached, part-written
*     GO 4
*     TUPLE          && expect  C4 | HIT | ZERO   M1 ran (section 1, silent)
*                    &&                           M2 did NOT (section 2 bailed)
*
* M2 ON RECORD 4 IS THE DISCRIMINATOR. Without the bail-out it reads HIT.
* Its neighbour M1 reading HIT on the same row is the control: it proves the
* scan machinery reaches record 4 perfectly well when nothing stops it.
*
* Self-bootstrapping and self-erasing in DBF/SANDBOX. Leaves STOP_ON_ERROR set
* to WARNING because the script cannot reach its own reset line -- a
* consequence of the ruling working. Reset with STOP_ON_ERROR OFF.
* ===========================================================================

SETPATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX

ERASE SCERR CONFIRM

SELECT 0
CREATE X64 SCERR (CK C(4), M1 C(8), M2 C(8))
APPEND
REPLACE CK WITH "C1"
REPLACE M1 WITH "ZERO"
REPLACE M2 WITH "ZERO"
APPEND
REPLACE CK WITH "C2"
REPLACE M1 WITH "ZERO"
REPLACE M2 WITH "ZERO"
APPEND
REPLACE CK WITH "C3"
REPLACE M1 WITH "ZERO"
REPLACE M2 WITH "ZERO"
APPEND
REPLACE CK WITH "C4"
REPLACE M1 WITH "ZERO"
REPLACE M2 WITH "ZERO"

SELECT SCERR
GO 4
? "SCE_G1_fixture_row4_is_C4:" + (ALLTRIM(CK) = "C4")
GO 4
? "SCE_G2_fixture_row4_starts_ZERO:" + (ALLTRIM(M2) = "ZERO")

STOP_ON_ERROR WARNING

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* SECTION 1 -- THE SILENT FAILURE. No bail expected. TRIPWIRE.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

GO 1
SCAN
REPLACE M1 WITH "HIT"
REPLACE NOFIELD WITH "X"
ENDSCAN

GO 4
? "SCE_T1_replace_failure_is_INVISIBLE_all_rows_ran:" + (ALLTRIM(M1) = "HIT")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* SECTION 2 -- A RECORDED ERROR. The bail IS expected, and takes the script.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

GO 1
SCAN
REPLACE M2 WITH "HIT"
SET ERRORSTOP TO BOGUS
ENDSCAN

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* NOT REACHED ON A PASSING RUN. An arm that proves its own absence is worth
* more than a comment saying it should be absent.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

? "SCE_X1_script_did_NOT_stop_THIS_SHOULD_NOT_PRINT:" + ("A" = "A")

STOP_ON_ERROR OFF
